Not only did the investors miss the revolution ahead of time the economists
missed the fact as it was happening around them. Even good ones, Adam Smith
comes to mind.

Similarly even now B-school simulations don't have a winning outcome based
on positive-sum outcomes. IMHO We need a new field, a collalary to
economics that deals with the creative side of wealth creation. Moore's law
(and it's generalizations) make a good cornerstone.

 [Gordon Moore, founder of Intel noticed that CPU_operations_per_dollar
doubles   
 about every 2 years and has been doing so for over 40 years. ie. a 
 million-to-one increase.]

Though not strictly Moore's law I've noticed that after 40 years the
doubling time for mass storage has dropped from 2 years to 16-18 months. My
guess is that general recognition of the phenominon has motivated all the
hard disk players to try really hard to get slightly ahead of the curve.
(Anyone have a better explanation?)

Unfortunately there comes a point where the market collectively says  "I'll
wait to buy because next month everything will be better and cost less".
Then profits evaporate. Then a shakeout occurs. This is too bad because I
find it really fun to create products in the fastest moving times. 



