Simon is the leading researcher on the subject, that's all.  I actually
haven't read anything by him, because his writing style is too dry for
me.  I prefer what David Osterfeld wrote on this subject, explaining how
resources are increasing relative to population, have been for quite some
time now, and show all signs of continuing to do so in the future.

BTW, referring to our point of view as "dogma" is hardly flattering, or
likely to get you much of a hearing.

As far as I know, Simon hasn't made any of the errors you list, except
perhaps one that I'd like you to explain a little better so I can figure
out what you're talking about:

>3. The error of believing that human societies can distribute resources
>optimally.  There will always be large inefficiencies in a complex social
>or economic system.

As a matter of fact, I not only take the position that human societies can
optimally distribute resources, but that they do so naturally in the
absence of coercive interference.  How is this erroneous?

>...My confidence is not inspired by the arrogant, overconfident way the
>Simonites speak of him.

That's just their own personal style, that's all.  Don't let it get to you.

>Let me get back to Dave's letter:
>
>>>4.  The Earth will soon have too many humans.
>>
>>No way.  P. J. O'Rourke, in his newest book _All The Trouble In The World_,
>>points out that the entire population of the Earth could live in Texas and
>>Oklahoma at the population density of present-day San Francisco (calculate
>>how much land that leaves left over for agriculture -- about 100%, I'd
>>say).
>
>Ask a city planner about building a city the size of Texas and Oklahoma
>with the population density of San Francisco.

What, exactly, is the pop. density of SF?  I'm there right now, and I don't
know that.  The population varies from about 2.5 mil. at noon weekdays to
about 650 thou. midnight, but I don't know how many square miles SF has.

But the particular example shouldn't matter.  Bucky Fuller designed cities
to house 100,000 within a 1-square mile radius, and Hong Kong's pop.
density gets up to about 700,000/mi2 as I figure it.  Besides, those who
argue that we'll run out of living space soon tend to commit some errors
of their own:

1) We can only live on land.  We can't live on water, in the air, under-
ground, etc.

2) We can only live on this planet.  We can't colonize the Moon, Mars, etc.

3) "Open space" has some intrinsic value over and above what value humans
place upon it.

As for your scientific points, I'll just refer you to Ronald Bailey's
"EcoScam," along with Michael Fumento's "Science Under Siege," unless you
would like to stick to just one issue at a time.

The last thing worth noting is that the worst pollution occurs with those
things that aren't privately owned.  A conceptually clear solution to this
problem is to privatize them, although this may be difficult in practice.

