From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Thu, 24 Nov 94 00:53:30 EST
Subject: [#94-11-342] GENE: Resurrecting the dodo? 

>I have thought about the "Jurassic Park scenario" lately, where DNA from
>an extinct species is recreated, multiplied by PCR, inserted into living
>eggs and hatched.... So, what species might be good candidates for a
>resurrecting experiment? 
>
>My favorite is the dodo, for several reasons. 
>....
>Any comments on this scheme? What are the technical and scientific
>problems? How big resources would be needed to resurrect a few
>individuals? 
>

It is an interesting idea. One must be careful that resurrection using DNA
is not viewed as a panacea. Here are some possible problems with DNA based
resurrection.

1) The precise chemical constitution of the egg or intrauterine environment
       may be unknown. Yet this microenvironment can be very important
       topobiologically.

2) The newly created creature will not be exposed to the socialization 
       and meme transference of a mature parent or parents.

3) The newly created creature will not be exposed to the socialization and
       meme transference of larger social groupings, e.g., flock, pride,
       tribe.

Attempts can be made to reconstitute this lost information. Direct analysis
of the DNA of the extinct species and related species may allow one to
construct a prenatal environment which is survivable. A resurrected adult
female would probably allow one to acquire more accurate knowledge of the
prenatal environment so you can bootstrap.

Extinction halts the direct transfer of memes and it appears more difficult
to accurately reinstate the results of memetic evolution. Maybe one can
examine the behavior of related creatures and build a robotic substitute
parent to transfer plausible memes. This is not ideal and some information
may be unrecoverable.

Of course, not everyone values verisimilitude and some may be satisfied with
creatures that look but do not act like the original creatures.

The example of the dodo raises another problem.  I believe the dodo was a
flightless bird that was driven to extinction by predators which were
introduced to its island habitat. The resurrected dodo would probably suffer
a similar fate in a modern hostile environment. One may wish to craft an
entire historically accurate and thus more congenial island for the dodo.

