From: freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman)
Date: Wed, 23 Nov 1994 22:10:25 -0800
Subject: [#94-11-343] GENE: Resurrecting the dodo?

I suspect the reinstantiation of extinct species from DNA will be
last-in, first-out for much the kind of reasons commonly applied to
cryonically stored humans: Older DNA is likely in sadder shape and
might require more biologically clever preprocessing.  Perhaps the
most likely early candidates for reinstantiation would be species
which had been correctly identified as in serious trouble, and had DNA
samples taken, before extinction.  One candidate might be the
California Condor.  Another possible early application of this
technology might be to increase the genetic diversity of existing
species via DNA taken from populations or subspecies now extinct, or
in trouble.
 
