From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Thu, 24 Nov 94 02:29:00 EST
Subject: [#94-11-344] NANOTECH: Drexler predicts when it will happen 

Ed Regis says
> ...  The fact is that Drexler has long
>since abandoned the notion that self-replication is necessary for molecular
>manufacturing. (You won't find much about self-replication in Nanosystems.)
>What's important is massive parallelism, not the ability to self-replicate.
> If one assembler can be built through Brownian assembly (nee
>"self-assembly"), then billions of them can be built that way in parallel.
> Then, so long as these billions of parallel assemblers can be made to work
>in concert, then molecular manufacturing can proceed, and it can proceed
>economically.  Neither exponential growth nor self-replication is a necessary
>condition of nanotechnology.

I think Ed Regis is correct when he states that much can be accomplished in
nanotechnology without self-replication.  The description he gives above
corresponds to what Drexler calls Stage One systems I believe.  However, in
Nanosystems Drexler calls replication ``a key threshold in development''.
It is used to achieve Stage Two and Three systems.  Much of Nanosysystems is
about diamondoid material systems which Drexler indicates are Stage Three.

Consider Chapter 16 which is entitled ``Paths to Molecular Manufacturing''.
Drexler outlines the stages as follows:

Stage 1a: Brownian assembly of medium scale blocks
Stage 1b: Mechanosynthetic assembly of small building blocks
Stage 2: First-generation solution-based systems
Stage 3: Inert environments, diamondoid materials

The following quote is from Nanosystems from Section 16.5.4 page 487 which
discusses Stage 2. 

The estimated time required for a system of this kind to build a copy of
itself (a key threshold in development) is several hours. If replication
time is ~ 1 hour (Section 16.4.4c), then the time required to produce a
macroscopic quantity of systems is several days. Subsequent production of
other devices can supply relatively low-cost, disposable laboratories for
mechanosynthesis. The cost of required reagents is negligible on a
per-device basis. For example, a cost of 10^4 $/gm amounts to ~$10^-12 per
complex device. New reagents, operations, and devices can be developed in
parallel in many laboratories. From this technology base, the direction of
advance is toward enclosed systems with larger manipulators, then toward
inert interiors, then toward more active reagents. Experimental cycle
times can remain short throughout this process.

End quote

Stage Two and Three systems are important in Nanosystems so I think
it is accurate to state that replication is important in Nanosystems.

There are actually nuances to the meaning of the term replicate.  Some
systems Drexler describes are not really self-contained self-replicators.
For example, Drexler has clever ideas for communicating with systems using
modulated pressure via mechanical transducers (pg 472). These systems do not
need to directly incorporate computers and stored instructions. Instead, the
computers and instructions remain at the macroscale level. Therefore the
systems are not self-contained self-replicators because the computers and
instructions are not replicated.  However, for Stage Three systems he states
that one can start to internalize control and data, etc. 
