Phil Goetz suggests that maybe Julian Simon is the *only* person to 
disagree with the highly publicized eco-doomsters. This, of course, is far 
from the truth. We tend to recommend Simon's work not because it's 
unique, but because he has written plenty, expressed his ideas extremely 
clearly, and has a strong economics background. He writes on a wide 
range of environmental issues, rather than specializing in one or two. 
Dave (I believe it was) mentioned Herman Kahn as another eco-critic. 
You don't hear about many of the critics because the press rarely gives 
them a voice.

Here are just some of the eco-rationalists and critics I could cite:

Bruce N. Ames, prof. of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Univ. of 
California, Berkely.

David B. Baker, Director, Water Quality Laboratory, Heidelberg College, 
formerly prof. of Biology at Heidelberg.

William J. Baumol, Prof. of Economics at Princeton and at New York 
University, past President of the American Economics Association.

Petr Beckman (author of The Health Hazards of Not Going Nuclear, and 
publisher of Access To Energy). Sadly, recently deceased.

William M. Brown, Director of Energy and Technological Studies at the 
Hudson Institute.

Marion Clawson, agricultural economist.

Bernard L. Cohen, Prof. of Physics at the Univ. of Pittsburgh.

Robert M. Devlin, professor of Plant Physiology and Weed Science at the 
Univ. of Massachusetts.

J. Gordon Edwards, prof. of biology at San Jose State University.

Dr., Hugh W. Ellsaesser of the Lawrence Livermore National 
Laboratory, and formerly weather officer for the United States Air Force.

Richard S. Fawcett, consultant and formerly professor of agronomy at 
Iowa State University.

John Fraser Hart, Prof. of Geography at the University of Minnesota.

D. Gale Johnson, Professor and Chairman of the Dept. of Economics at 
the Unv. of Chicago.

H. E. Landsberg, Professor Emeritus at the Institute for Fluid Dynamics 
and Applied Mathematics, University of Maryland.


Jane M. Orient, executive director, Association of American Physicians 
and Surgeons.

Richard Peto, Imperial Cancer Research Fund Reader in Cancer Studies, 
University of Oxford.

Roger Revelle, Professor of Science and Public Policy at the University of 
San Diego.

S. Fred Singer, geophysicist, Professor of Environmental Sciences at the 
